{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Resource Owner Password Grant.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Resource Owner Password Grant.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"resource owner password grant resource owner password grant","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Resource Owner Password Grant.md","fileName":"Resource Owner Password Grant.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Resource Owner Password Grant","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 22:09\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[OAuth 2.0 and IdentityServer]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["OAuth 2.0 and IdentityServer","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Resource Owner Password Grant.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Resource Owner Password Grant.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"resource owner password grant resource owner password grant","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Resource Owner Password Grant.md","fileName":"Resource Owner Password Grant.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Resource Owner Password Grant","heading":"Resource Owner Password Grant","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The resource owner password grant sends a user's username and password from a trusted client to the authorization service in exchange for an access token. The book uses it for a constrained 2019 desktop application that must work with a locally hosted identity service.\n\nThe flow makes the client responsible for collecting credentials and can expose an embedded client secret through reverse engineering. The chapter therefore treats it as a tradeoff for its offline-oriented scenario and points toward a hosted authentication interface as a safer design for broader access.","tags":["baby"],"links":["OAuth 2.0 and IdentityServer","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf"],"properties":[],"p